  <footer class="provenance">
    <h2 class="prov-h">Sources, method and revisions</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <h6>Source of record</h6>
        <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. "Estimates of Gross Domestic Product for the Second Quarter (July–September) of 2025–26." Press release, 28 November 2025. Constant-price levels retrieved via the eSankhyiki API, vintage <b id="prov-vintage" set:text={T["prov-vintage"]}></b>; growth rates are computed from levels, not taken from the rounded published rates.</p>
        <p>The expenditure-side series, government consumption, exports, imports, and the annual accounts (NDP, GNI, taxes and subsidies on products, capital consumption, capital formation) are drawn from the same NAS releases, same vintage. The annual income-side accounts are not published quarterly; their detailed lines end two fiscal years behind the headline, and that gap is shown, not filled.</p>
        <p>Version 1.9 (October 2026) takes in every row the API serves for the national account: every indicator on both bases at constant and current prices, every estimate stage of every year, GVA by 15 industry rows and 18 sub-industries, capital formation by industry of use, saving, investment, value added and depreciation by institutional sector, quarterly levels, and the NSO back series' full accounts from 1950-51. Shares, growth, net national income and the discrepancy are computed from published levels. Base 2022–23 levels were checked by hand against MoSPI's press release of 31 August 2026 (National Accounts Statistics 2026): real GDP of ₹3,01,15,428 crore for 2024-25 and ₹3,24,70,100 crore for 2025-26, exact. That release restated every new-base year for the new producer price and industrial production indices; the API holds the restated figure under the old stage label. Three source defects are worked around and disclosed: taxes and subsidies labelled ₹ lakh but published in ₹ crore; MoSPI's growth rates in the API sometimes cut rather than rounded; and an approach cut the data endpoint ignores.</p>
        <p>Version 1.8 widened the intake to the rest of what the NAS tables serve: industry-wise real GVA for the eight sector groups (quarterly, with the release's own rounded growth rates kept as a cross-check), nominal GDP and the implied deflator (current-price over constant-price levels), the gross saving rate (saving is published at current prices only, so it appears as a share of nominal GDP), and the new 2022–23-base quarterly series as its own bundle: the two bases are never spliced. Q3 and Q4 of FY26, from the 27 February and 29 May 2026 releases, exist only on the new base. Not covered because the source does not serve it: national per-capita income: the API's per-capita indicators exist state-wise only, and this guide will not derive one from a population guess.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Revisions &amp; uncertainty</h6>
        <p>National accounts are revised continuously: quarterly estimates are restated with each new release and finalised over roughly three years. This guide quotes vintages explicitly; where estimates conflict, both are shown.</p>
        <p>Where something is not measured, this guide says "we do not know" rather than inventing a number.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>This guide</h6>
        <p>Output, version 1.9, adds where the money goes, income from abroad, the discrepancy, who saves and invests, the eighteen sub-industries, how a GDP number is revised, and what India has made since 1950-51. Next scheduled revision: the Q2 FY27 print, due at the end of November 2026.</p>
      </div>
